<?php

namespace Database\Seeders\Demo;

return [
    '2nd Baccalaureate · Demo' => [
        'description' => 'English for Moroccan 2nd Baccalaureate learners. Develop the reading, language and writing skills used in the national exam through school and community scenarios.',
        'units' => [
            'Gifts of youth' => [
                'description' => 'Report what young people do for their communities and explain why their contributions matter.',
                'lessons' => [
                    ['Grammar', 'Past participles', 'Distinguish the simple past from the past participle.', 'A simple past verb can stand alone: “Meryem wrote a report.” After have or a passive form of be, use the past participle: “Meryem has written a report” and “The report was written yesterday.” Regular verbs usually share the same form in both columns (organised, collected). Learn irregular forms in groups: write–wrote–written, send–sent–sent, take–took–taken.', 'Circle the verbs in “The club took photos” and “The photos were taken at noon.” Which one follows be? Then make a similar pair using choose–chose–chosen.'],
                    ['Grammar', 'The passive voice', 'Choose between active and passive voice and preserve tense and agreement.', 'Use the passive to focus on the receiver or result: “The volunteers repaired two bicycles” becomes “Two bicycles were repaired.” Move the object to subject position, select the form of be that agrees with it and carries the original tense, then add the past participle. Include a by-phrase only when the agent adds useful information. “The hall is cleaned every Friday” describes a routine. “The hall was cleaned yesterday” describes one past event.', 'Write two lines for the school bulletin: “Students collected 40 books on Monday. Someone will deliver the books on Friday.” Make the books the focus of both lines.', 'Au passif, c’est be qui porte le temps. Avec un sujet pluriel au passé, utilise were.', 'في المبني للمجهول نُقدّم الشيء الذي وقع عليه الفعل، ونستعمل صيغة مناسبة من be ثم اسم المفعول.'],
                    ['Vocabulary', 'From talent to contribution', 'Describe a young person’s strengths and show how they benefit others.', 'A skill is something a person can do, such as repairing a bicycle. A strength is a quality or ability that helps them do it well, such as patience. A contribution is what they give to the group: time, equipment or a useful idea. Common combinations include develop a skill, show initiative and make a contribution. “Yassine showed initiative by asking the library to host a repair workshop.”', 'Write a two-sentence nomination for a classmate. Use one strength and one specific action as evidence.'],
                    ['Functions', 'Making suggestions in a club meeting', 'Propose a practical action and respond constructively.', 'Use “Why don’t we + base verb?” to offer a joint action and “How about + -ing?” to invite discussion. A useful response names a constraint and offers an adjustment: “That could work, but the hall is booked. Could we meet in the library instead?” Use “Let’s…” when the group is ready to agree on a next step.', 'In pairs, plan a book exchange with a budget of 50 dirhams. Each partner makes a suggestion, raises one constraint and agrees on the next action.'],
                    ['Reading', 'A Saturday repair café', 'Find evidence for an inference about a volunteer project.', 'Read: “The Tétouan youth club opened its repair café with two screwdrivers and a borrowed table. Only three people visited on the first Saturday. By the fourth Saturday, families were queuing before ten. The volunteers began handing out numbered slips so visitors could return later.” The final sentence never says the café was busy, but the queue and slips are evidence for that inference. An inference should explain the clues and stay within what the passage supports.', 'Find two details showing the café changed over time. Write one inference and cite a detail. Does the text support a claim that repairs were free? Explain.'],
                    ['Writing', 'A short report for the school newsletter', 'Write a factual report with a clear lead and specific evidence.', 'Begin with who, what, where and when: “On Saturday, the English club held a book exchange in the school library.” Add a measurable result before giving background: “Forty books changed hands.” Attribute an opinion to its speaker and avoid unsupported praise. A final sentence can tell readers what will happen next.', 'Write a 90-word report about a clothing drive. Use these notes: 18 volunteers, 65 coats, collection in the courtyard, delivery next Tuesday. Underline the fact that belongs in the lead.'],
                ],
            ],
            'Sustainable development' => [
                'description' => 'Read about local environmental decisions, propose changes and support them with evidence.',
                'lessons' => [
                    ['Vocabulary', 'Water, waste and everyday choices', 'Use precise collocations to describe a conservation problem and solution.', 'To conserve water is to avoid wasting it. To reuse a bottle is to use it again, while recycling turns material into something new. A refill station helps reduce single-use plastic by making reuse easier. “The school could repair leaking taps to reduce water waste.” Notice the verb + noun pairs: repair a leak, reduce waste, conserve water.', 'List two problems you can observe at school. For each one write an action using a different collocation and say what you could measure after one week.'],
                    ['Functions', 'Giving advice with a reason', 'Distinguish a suggestion from a requirement and explain the difference politely.', 'Should is useful for advice: “The club should label each bin so students know where things go.” Must indicates an obligation: “Visitors must keep the emergency exit clear.” For collaborative proposals, start with “We could…” and explain the likely benefit using because or so that.', 'Advise a school that wants to reduce litter. Give one suggestion, one obligation concerning safety and a reason for each.'],
                    ['Reading', 'What the refill station changed', 'Distinguish an observed change from a causal claim.', 'Read: “In September a lycée in Agadir recorded 120 disposable bottles in its courtyard bins over five school days. In October it installed a refill station. A second five-day count found 74 bottles. The October count took place during a week when one year group was on a field trip.” The count fell by 46 bottles, but attendance changed too. We need comparable attendance or a bottles-per-student measure before assigning the change to the station.', 'Underline the figures and circle the detail that limits the comparison. Write a cautious conclusion and propose one way to improve the next count.'],
                    ['Grammar', 'First conditional for practical plans', 'Describe a realistic future condition and consequence.', 'Use if + present simple for the condition and will + base verb for a predicted result: “If the bins are clearly labelled, more students will sort their waste.” Put a comma after an opening if-clause. Use may instead of will when the result is uncertain: “We may save water if we repair the taps.”', 'Write two forecasts about a school garden, one likely and one uncertain. Swap the clauses in one sentence and check the punctuation.', 'Après if, emploie le présent simple pour une condition future réalisable.', 'نستعمل المضارع البسيط بعد if عندما يكون الشرط ممكناً في المستقبل، ثم نعبّر عن النتيجة باستعمال will أو may.'],
                    ['Writing', 'An evidence-based proposal', 'Write a short proposal with a feasible action and a way to measure it.', 'Open with the observed problem, then propose one practical change. Use evidence precisely: “We counted 74 bottles in five days” gives the reader more information than “There is a lot of plastic.” Add who will do what and when, and state how success will be measured. End with a specific request for approval or support.', 'Write 100–120 words to your headteacher proposing a two-week refill-station trial. Specify a baseline, a follow-up measure and who will collect it.'],
                ],
            ],
            'Citizenship' => [
                'description' => 'Read community notices, report what people said and write a respectful request.',
                'lessons' => [
                    ['Vocabulary', 'Rights, responsibilities and participation', 'Use civic vocabulary in everyday school and community contexts.', 'A right is something people are entitled to, while a responsibility is a duty they are expected to fulfil. Participation means taking an active part. A student can exercise the right to express an opinion and meet the responsibility to listen respectfully. Volunteers can raise awareness by making a problem better known.', 'Describe one school right and the responsibility that helps everyone exercise it. Then write a sentence using “raise awareness of”.'],
                    ['Reading', 'Reading a neighbourhood consultation notice', 'Identify audience, deadline and practical instructions in a public notice.', 'Read: “Residents of Hay Salam are invited to comment on plans for the community library. Written suggestions must reach the youth centre by 5 p.m. on Thursday. Students under 18 may submit suggestions. The Saturday meeting will summarise proposals already received.” The deadline applies to written suggestions. The Saturday date is for the summary meeting.', 'Write a two-line reminder to a classmate who thinks the Saturday meeting is the deadline. Include the delivery location and exact time.'],
                    ['Grammar', 'Reported speech in a meeting summary', 'Report a statement and a yes/no question accurately.', 'When reporting a past conversation, change pronouns from the new speaker’s perspective. Backshift is common when the situation has changed: “I need a larger room,” Hiba said on Monday becomes “Hiba said she needed a larger room.” For a yes/no question use asked + if/whether + subject + verb: “Is the hall open?” becomes “She asked whether the hall was open.” Use statement word order after whether.', 'Report these lines from yesterday’s meeting: “We have ten volunteers,” the organiser said. “Can we meet tomorrow?” a member asked. Identify who we refers to before changing it.'],
                    ['Functions', 'Agreeing and disagreeing respectfully', 'Acknowledge a point and offer a specific reason for a different view.', 'Separate the person from the proposal. “I see why Saturday works for your team. I’d prefer Friday because more students can attend after class.” A partial agreement can identify shared ground: “I agree that we need more space, but I’m unsure that the gym is available.” Ask a follow-up question if you need a fact before deciding.', 'Compare two locations for a school exhibition. Before disagreeing, restate one point your partner made. Then ask one question about the venue.'],
                    ['Writing', 'A formal request to a local association', 'Write a concise email with a specific request, supporting details and a polite close.', 'Use a subject that states the purpose: “Request to use the meeting room on 14 March.” Open with “Dear…” and make the request in the first paragraph: “Could our English club use your meeting room from 3 to 4 p.m.?” Include the group size and explain what you will need. End with thanks and a contact name.', 'Write an email asking an association to lend six tables for a student exhibition. Give the date, collection plan and return time. Check that the recipient could answer without asking for basic details.', 'Dans un courriel formel, place la demande précise dès le premier paragraphe.'],
                ],
            ],
        ],
    ],
    'Everyday English · A2–B1' => [
        'description' => 'Build confidence for practical conversations, from finding a place to following up on an appointment. Parcours complémentaire pour communiquer au quotidien.',
        'units' => [
            'Finding your way' => [
                'description' => 'Ask for directions, follow a short route and confirm travel details.',
                'lessons' => [
                    ['Vocabulary', 'Landmarks and route instructions', 'Use landmarks and location phrases to make directions precise.', 'A landmark is a place someone can recognise along the route. “Turn left at the bakery” gives a turning point. “The clinic is opposite the post office” locates the destination across the street. “Go past the pharmacy” means continue beyond it. Use on your left for the traveller’s viewpoint.', 'Sketch a street with a bakery, pharmacy and clinic. Give a partner three instructions and ask them to mark the destination.', 'En face de se traduit par opposite ou across from.'],
                    ['Functions', 'Checking directions politely', 'Ask for directions and confirm a detail before leaving.', 'Start with “Excuse me, could you tell me how to get to the station?” If you miss a detail, ask “Sorry, did you say the first or the second turning?” Finish by repeating the essential instruction: “So I go past the bakery and turn right at the bank?”', 'Role-play a visitor who must reach the station before noon. Ask for directions, check one detail and repeat the route back.', null, 'للتأكد من الاتجاه، أعد صياغة الجزء المهم من التعليمات واسأل إن كنت قد فهمته بشكل صحيح.'],
                    ['Reading', 'A station notice after a platform change', 'Find the replacement platform and distinguish it from the departure time.', 'Read: “The 10:25 service to Rabat will leave from platform 3 today. Passengers waiting on platform 1 should use the footbridge. Step-free access to platform 3 is available via the lift next to the ticket office.” The destination and departure time are unchanged. Travellers who need step-free access should locate the lift.', 'Write a text message to a friend waiting on platform 1. Include the new platform, departure time and the step-free route.'],
                ],
            ],
            'Appointments and follow-up' => [
                'description' => 'Arrange, change and confirm an appointment using short, courteous messages.',
                'lessons' => [
                    ['Grammar', 'Present continuous for arrangements', 'Distinguish a confirmed arrangement from an intention.', 'Use am/is/are + -ing with a future time for arrangements made with another person: “I’m meeting the adviser on Tuesday at four.” Use going to for an intention without a fixed booking: “I’m going to speak to an adviser this month.” A time phrase shows that the first example refers to the future.', 'Write one intention and one confirmed arrangement for next week. Exchange sentences and highlight the details showing which one is fixed.'],
                    ['Writing', 'Rescheduling an appointment', 'Name the original appointment, offer alternatives and ask for confirmation.', '“I’m writing about our appointment on Tuesday at 3 p.m.” identifies the booking. Briefly explain the change and offer two specific alternatives: “Could we meet on Wednesday after 4 p.m. or Thursday morning?” Close with “Please let me know which time works for you.” Wait for confirmation before assuming the appointment has moved.', 'Write a 60-word message changing a library induction. Include the original slot and two feasible alternatives.', null, 'اذكر الموعد الأصلي واقترح وقتين بديلين، ثم اطلب تأكيد الموعد الجديد.'],
                ],
            ],
        ],
    ],
];
